## Solution

**Setup.** Let $\deg P = n$ with leading coefficient $a \neq 0$. Then
$$P(P(x)) \sim a^{n+1}x^{n^2}, \qquad (P(x))^{16} \sim a^{16}x^{16n}.$$

For the leading terms of $P(P(x)) - (P(x))^{16}$ to cancel (so the result can be $Q(x)+x^{48}$ rather than a higher-degree polynomial), we need $n^2 = 16n$, giving $n = 16$, and $a^{17} = a^{16}$, giving $a = 1$.

So write $P(x) = x^{16} + p(x)$ with $\deg p \le 15$ (or $p = 0$).

**Key identity.** Since $P(x) = x^{16} + p(x)$,
$$P(P(x)) = (P(x))^{16} + p(P(x)),$$
hence
$$P(P(x)) - (P(x))^{16} = p(P(x)) = Q(x) + x^{48}.$$

**Determining $p$.** If $p \neq 0$ with $\deg p = d$ and leading coefficient $c$, then $p(P(x)) \sim c\, x^{16d}$. To produce the $x^{48}$ term we need $16d = 48$, i.e. $d = 3$, and $c = 1$. (If $p = 0$, then $Q = -x^{48}$, degree $48$, which is worse.)

So $p(x) = x^{3} + q(x)$ with $\deg q \le 2$ (or $q = 0$).

**Expanding $Q$.** We have
$$Q(x) = p(P(x)) - x^{48} = (P(x))^{3} + q(P(x)) - x^{48}.$$

Now $(P(x))^{3} = (x^{16} + p(x))^{3} = x^{48} + 3x^{32}p(x) + 3x^{16}p(x)^{2} + p(x)^{3}$, so
$$Q(x) = \underbrace{3x^{32}p(x)}_{\deg 35} + \underbrace{3x^{16}p(x)^{2}}_{\deg 22} + \underbrace{p(x)^{3}}_{\deg 9} + \underbrace{q(P(x))}_{\deg \le 32}.$$

The **degree-$35$ term** comes solely from $3x^{32}p(x)$: its $x^{35}$ coefficient is $3 \cdot (\text{coeff of } x^{3} \text{ in } p) = 3 \cdot 1 = 3$. The lower-degree terms of $p$ (degree $\le 2$) only affect degrees $\le 34$ within $3x^{32}p(x)$, and all other summands have degree $\le 32$. Thus the $x^{35}$ coefficient of $Q$ is exactly $3 \neq 0$, and cannot be canceled by any choice of $q$.

Therefore $\deg Q = 35$.

**Achievability.** Take $p(x) = x^{3}$, i.e. $P(x) = x^{16} + x^{3}$. Then
$$Q(x) = (x^{16}+x^{3})^{3} - x^{48} = 3x^{35} + 3x^{22} + x^{9},$$
which has degree $35$. ✓

**The minimum degree of $Q(x)$ is $\boxed{35}$.**

